# The Mac's LAN address, not localhost.
#
# On a physical iPhone `localhost` resolves to the phone itself, so every request
# hit a closed port on the device and the app looked like it could not log in. The
# simulator is the misleading case: it shares the Mac's network stack, so localhost
# works there and the bug only shows up on real hardware — which is also the only
# place the PT-210 printer can be tested at all.
#
# ⚠️ LAN addresses move (different wifi, a new DHCP lease). If login starts failing
# with a connection error rather than "invalid password", re-check it:
#     ipconfig getifaddr en0
# The Mac and the iPhone must be on the same network, and the backend must be
# listening on 0.0.0.0 rather than 127.0.0.1 to accept off-device connections.
# ⚠️ .env.local OVERRIDES THIS FILE. Expo loads .env.local after .env, so if
# both set EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL, the one in .env.local is the one the app
# uses and editing this line changes nothing. This cost a debugging session
# once: .env said one IP, .env.local said an older one, and the app kept
# reporting "Cannot reach TWD" against an address that appeared nowhere in the
# file being edited. Change BOTH, or change .env.local alone.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.12:5001/api


